%I A396700 #22 Sep 10 2026 23:30:51
%S A396700 2,3,5,7,8,10,11,13,15,17,21,23,24,26,27,28,31,35,37,43,46,47,48,50,
%T A396700 51,53,57,63,65,67,71,73,77,79,80,82,83,86,91,92,97,99,101,111,116,
%U A396700 119,120,122,123,124,125,127,133,143,145,157,163,165,166,167,168
%N A396700 Numbers k >= 2 such that sqrt(k*b + c) = sqrt(b) + c for some integers b >= 1 and 0 <= c < k.
%C A396700 Every solution has b = x^2 for some integer x >= 1.
%C A396700 Complete parametrization: fix x >= 1 and choose a unitary factorization x = r*s with gcd(r,s) = 1. Then c must lie in the unique residue class modulo x^2 determined by c == 0 (mod r^2), c == 1 - 2*x (mod s^2).
%C A396700 Every positive c in this class satisfying c >= (x-1)^2 gives a solution through the formula below, and every solution arises uniquely in this way.
%C A396700 Equivalently, if A is the representative with 0 <= A < x^2 of one of these Chinese remainder theorem residue classes, then c = A + t*x^2 for all integers t sufficiently large that c > 0 and c >= (x-1)^2.
%C A396700 For fixed x, the solutions split into exactly 2^omega(x) distinct infinite quadratic families, where omega(x) is the number of distinct prime divisors of x.
%C A396700 No base k can arise from two distinct pairs (c,b). Consequently, the quadratic families corresponding to different choices of x or different unitary factorizations are globally disjoint in k.
%C A396700 No perfect square occurs in the sequence.
%H A396700 Douglas E. Iannucci, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL3/iann2a.html">The Kaprekar numbers</a>, J. Integer Sequences, Vol. 3, 2000, #1.2.
%H A396700 V. Oxman and M. Stupel, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/mag.2024.122">A number whose square root is the sum of its digits</a>, Mathematical Gazette, 108 (573) (2024), 513-514.
%H A396700 D. Suryanarayana and V. Siva Rama Prasad, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-17-4-345-354">The number of k-free divisors of an integer</a>, Acta Arithmetica, 17 (1971), 345-354.
%F A396700 a(n) ~ (Pi^4/9)*n^2/(log n)^4.
%e A396700 10 is a term because, taking b = 16 and c = 9, sqrt(10*16 + 9) = sqrt(169) = 13 = sqrt(16) + 9.
%e A396700 The first term 2 is obtained from b = 4 and c = 1: sqrt(2*4 + 1) = 3 = sqrt(4) + 1.
%e A396700 For x = 1 there is one quadratic family, c = t, k = t^2 + t + 1, t >= 1, which gives 3, 7, 13, 21, 31, 43, ...
%e A396700 For x = 2 there are two quadratic families, c = 4*t - 3, k = 4*t^2 - 3*t + 1, and c = 4*t, k = 4*t^2 + 3*t + 1, with t >= 1.
%o A396700 (Python)
%o A396700 from math import isqrt
%o A396700 def admissible(k):
%o A396700     for x in range(1, isqrt(k - 1) + 2):
%o A396700         D = 4*k*x*x - 4*x + 1
%o A396700         d = isqrt(D)
%o A396700         if d*d != D:
%o A396700             continue
%o A396700         num = d - (2*x - 1)
%o A396700         if num % 2:
%o A396700             continue
%o A396700         c = num // 2
%o A396700         if 1 <= c < k and k*x*x + c == (x+c)**2:
%o A396700             return True
%o A396700     return False
%o A396700 print([k for k in range(2, 200) if admissible(k)])
%K A396700 nonn,new
%O A396700 1,1
